1
00:00:03,917 --> 00:00:08,237
Now that I was left wholly to myself, I gave notice of my intention to quit

2
00:00:08,237 --> 00:00:12,758
the chambers in the Temple as soon as my tenancy could legally determine,

3
00:00:12,758 --> 00:00:19,160
and in the meanwhile to underlet them. At once I put bills up in the windows; for, I was in debt,

4
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:25,796
and had scarcely any money, and began to be seriously alarmed by the state of my affairs.

5
00:00:25,796 --> 00:00:30,078
I ought rather to write that I should have been alarmed if I had had energy and concentration

6
00:00:30,078 --> 00:00:34,078
enough to help me to the clear perception of any truth beyond the fact that I was falling

7
00:00:34,078 --> 00:00:42,000
very ill. The late stress upon me had enabled me to put off illness, but not to put it away; I knew

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:49,358
that it was coming on me now, and I knew very little else, and was even careless as to that.

9
00:00:49,358 --> 00:00:54,756
For a day or two, I lay on the sofa, or on the floor,—anywhere, according as I happened to

10
00:00:54,758 --> 00:01:03,917
sink down,—with a heavy head and aching limbs, and no purpose, and no power. Then there came,

11
00:01:03,917 --> 00:01:09,637
one night which appeared of great duration, and which teemed with anxiety and horror;

12
00:01:09,637 --> 00:01:15,637
and when in the morning I tried to sit up in my bed and think of it, I found I could not do so.

13
00:01:15,637 --> 00:01:20,519
Whether I really had been down in Garden Court in the dead of the night, groping about for the boat

14
00:01:20,519 --> 00:01:26,199
that I supposed to be there; whether I had two or three times come to myself on the staircase with

15
00:01:26,199 --> 00:01:32,839
great terror, not knowing how I had got out of bed; whether I had found myself lighting the lamp,

16
00:01:32,839 --> 00:01:38,398
possessed by the idea that he was coming up the stairs, and that the lights were blown out;

17
00:01:38,398 --> 00:01:43,237
whether I had been inexpressibly harassed by the distracted talking, laughing,

18
00:01:43,237 --> 00:01:50,277
and groaning of some one, and had half suspected those sounds to be of my own making;

19
00:01:50,277 --> 00:01:54,635
whether there had been a closed iron furnace in a dark corner of the room,

20
00:01:54,637 --> 00:01:59,839
and a voice had called out, over and over again, that Miss Havisham was consuming

21
00:01:59,839 --> 00:02:05,758
within it,—these were things that I tried to settle with myself and get into some order,

22
00:02:05,758 --> 00:02:12,476
as I lay that morning on my bed. But the vapour of a limekiln would come between me and them,

23
00:02:12,476 --> 00:02:20,038
disordering them all, and it was through the vapour at last that I saw two men looking at me.

24
00:02:20,038 --> 00:02:24,637
“What do you want?” I asked, starting; “I don’t know you.”

25
00:02:24,637 --> 00:02:29,637
“Well, sir,” returned one of them, bending down and touching me on the shoulder,

26
00:02:29,637 --> 00:02:33,917
“this is a matter that you’ll soon arrange, I dare say, but you’re arrested.”

27
00:02:33,917 --> 00:02:35,358
“What is the debt?”

28
00:02:35,358 --> 00:02:40,358
“Hundred and twenty-three pound, fifteen, six. Jeweller’s account, I think.”

29
00:02:40,358 --> 00:02:41,917
“What is to be done?”

30
00:02:41,917 --> 00:02:46,277
“You had better come to my house,” said the man. “I keep a very nice house.”

31
00:02:46,277 --> 00:02:51,237
I made some attempt to get up and dress myself. When I next attended to them,

32
00:02:51,237 --> 00:02:56,879
they were standing a little off from the bed, looking at me. I still lay there.

33
00:02:56,879 --> 00:03:01,879
“You see my state,” said I. “I would come with you if I could;

34
00:03:01,879 --> 00:03:09,718
but indeed I am quite unable. If you take me from here, I think I shall die by the way.”

35
00:03:09,718 --> 00:03:14,796
Perhaps they replied, or argued the point, or tried to encourage me to believe that I was

36
00:03:14,796 --> 00:03:21,237
better than I thought. Forasmuch as they hang in my memory by only this one slender thread,

37
00:03:21,237 --> 00:03:25,718
I don’t know what they did, except that they forbore to remove me.

38
00:03:25,718 --> 00:03:32,398
That I had a fever and was avoided, that I suffered greatly, that I often lost my reason,

39
00:03:32,398 --> 00:03:38,199
that the time seemed interminable, that I confounded impossible existences with my own

40
00:03:38,199 --> 00:03:44,637
identity; that I was a brick in the house-wall, and yet entreating to be released from the giddy

41
00:03:44,637 --> 00:03:51,319
place where the builders had set me; that I was a steel beam of a vast engine, clashing and

42
00:03:51,319 --> 00:03:57,319
whirling over a gulf, and yet that I implored in my own person to have the engine stopped,

43
00:03:57,319 --> 00:04:03,796
and my part in it hammered off; that I passed through these phases of disease, I know of my

44
00:04:03,796 --> 00:04:12,237
own remembrance, and did in some sort know at the time. That I sometimes struggled with real people,

45
00:04:12,237 --> 00:04:17,319
in the belief that they were murderers, and that I would all at once comprehend that they meant to

46
00:04:17,319 --> 00:04:24,476
do me good, and would then sink exhausted in their arms, and suffer them to lay me down, I also knew

47
00:04:24,476 --> 00:04:32,519
at the time. But, above all, I knew that there was a constant tendency in all these people,—who,

48
00:04:32,519 --> 00:04:37,879
when I was very ill, would present all kinds of extraordinary transformations of the human face,

49
00:04:37,879 --> 00:04:44,358
and would be much dilated in size,—above all, I say, I knew that there was an extraordinary

50
00:04:44,358 --> 00:04:51,038
tendency in all these people, sooner or later, to settle down into the likeness of Joe.

51
00:04:51,038 --> 00:04:55,436
After I had turned the worst point of my illness, I began to notice that while all

52
00:04:55,438 --> 00:05:02,358
its other features changed, this one consistent feature did not change. Whoever came about me,

53
00:05:02,358 --> 00:05:08,557
still settled down into Joe. I opened my eyes in the night, and I saw, in the great

54
00:05:08,559 --> 00:05:15,199
chair at the bedside, Joe. I opened my eyes in the day, and, sitting on the window-seat,

55
00:05:15,199 --> 00:05:22,917
smoking his pipe in the shaded open window, still I saw Joe. I asked for cooling drink,

56
00:05:22,917 --> 00:05:29,277
and the dear hand that gave it me was Joe’s. I sank back on my pillow after drinking,

57
00:05:29,277 --> 00:05:35,237
and the face that looked so hopefully and tenderly upon me was the face of Joe.

58
00:05:35,237 --> 00:05:40,635
At last, one day, I took courage, and said, “Is it Joe?”

59
00:05:40,637 --> 00:05:43,000
And the dear old home-voice answered,

60
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,597
“Which it air, old chap.” “O Joe, you break my heart!

61
00:05:47,597 --> 00:05:54,519
Look angry at me, Joe. Strike me, Joe. Tell me of my ingratitude. Don’t be so good to me!”

62
00:05:54,519 --> 00:05:57,839
For Joe had actually laid his head down on the pillow at my side,

63
00:05:57,839 --> 00:06:02,000
and put his arm round my neck, in his joy that I knew him.

64
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,237
“Which dear old Pip, old chap,” said Joe,

65
00:06:04,237 --> 00:06:10,519
“you and me was ever friends. And when you’re well enough to go out for a ride—what larks!”

66
00:06:10,519 --> 00:06:15,116
After which, Joe withdrew to the window, and stood with his back towards me,

67
00:06:15,116 --> 00:06:21,237
wiping his eyes. And as my extreme weakness prevented me from getting up and going to him,

68
00:06:21,237 --> 00:06:29,917
I lay there, penitently whispering, “O God bless him! O God bless this gentle Christian man!”

69
00:06:29,917 --> 00:06:33,000
Joe’s eyes were red when I next found him beside me;

70
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,519
but I was holding his hand, and we both felt happy.

71
00:06:37,519 --> 00:06:39,438
“How long, dear Joe?”

72
00:06:39,438 --> 00:06:42,637
“Which you meantersay, Pip, how long have your illness lasted, dear old chap?”

73
00:06:43,718 --> 00:06:45,237
“Yes, Joe.”

74
00:06:45,237 --> 00:06:50,237
“It’s the end of May, Pip. To-morrow is the first of June.”

75
00:06:50,237 --> 00:06:53,319
“And have you been here all that time, dear Joe?”

76
00:06:53,319 --> 00:06:57,237
“Pretty nigh, old chap. For, as I says to Biddy when the news of your

77
00:06:57,237 --> 00:07:01,635
being ill were brought by letter, which it were brought by the post,

78
00:07:01,637 --> 00:07:07,438
and being formerly single he is now married though underpaid for a deal of walking and shoe-leather,

79
00:07:07,438 --> 00:07:12,637
but wealth were not a object on his part, and marriage were the great wish of his hart—”

80
00:07:13,237 --> 00:07:18,557
“It is so delightful to hear you, Joe! But I interrupt you in what you said to Biddy.”

81
00:07:18,559 --> 00:07:24,116
“Which it were,” said Joe, “that how you might be amongst strangers, and that how you and me having

82
00:07:24,116 --> 00:07:30,160
been ever friends, a wisit at such a moment might not prove unacceptabobble. And Biddy,

83
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:38,277
her word were, ‘Go to him, without loss of time.’ That,” said Joe, summing up with his judicial air,

84
00:07:38,277 --> 00:07:45,160
“were the word of Biddy. ‘Go to him,’ Biddy say, ‘without loss of time.’ In short,

85
00:07:45,160 --> 00:07:50,398
I shouldn’t greatly deceive you,” Joe added, after a little grave reflection, “if I

86
00:07:50,398 --> 00:07:56,597
represented to you that the word of that young woman were, ‘without a minute’s loss of time.’”

87
00:07:56,597 --> 00:08:01,637
There Joe cut himself short, and informed me that I was to be talked to in great moderation,

88
00:08:01,637 --> 00:08:05,597
and that I was to take a little nourishment at stated frequent times, whether I felt

89
00:08:05,597 --> 00:08:12,277
inclined for it or not, and that I was to submit myself to all his orders. So I kissed his hand,

90
00:08:12,277 --> 00:08:18,597
and lay quiet, while he proceeded to indite a note to Biddy, with my love in it.

91
00:08:18,597 --> 00:08:24,637
Evidently Biddy had taught Joe to write. As I lay in bed looking at him, it made me,

92
00:08:24,637 --> 00:08:29,680
in my weak state, cry again with pleasure to see the pride with which he set about

93
00:08:29,680 --> 00:08:36,796
his letter. My bedstead, divested of its curtains, had been removed, with me upon it,

94
00:08:36,796 --> 00:08:42,519
into the sitting-room, as the airiest and largest, and the carpet had been taken away,

95
00:08:43,116 --> 00:08:49,758
and the room kept always fresh and wholesome night and day. At my own writing-table, pushed into a

96
00:08:49,758 --> 00:08:55,680
corner and cumbered with little bottles, Joe now sat down to his great work, first choosing a pen

97
00:08:55,680 --> 00:09:01,358
from the pen-tray as if it were a chest of large tools, and tucking up his sleeves as if he were

98
00:09:01,358 --> 00:09:07,319
going to wield a crow-bar or sledgehammer. It was necessary for Joe to hold on heavily to the

99
00:09:07,319 --> 00:09:12,519
table with his left elbow, and to get his right leg well out behind him, before he could begin;

100
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:18,557
and when he did begin he made every downstroke so slowly that it might have been six feet long,

101
00:09:18,559 --> 00:09:24,637
while at every upstroke I could hear his pen spluttering extensively. He had a curious idea

102
00:09:24,637 --> 00:09:30,078
that the inkstand was on the side of him where it was not, and constantly dipped his pen into space,

103
00:09:30,078 --> 00:09:34,637
and seemed quite satisfied with the result. Occasionally, he was tripped

104
00:09:34,637 --> 00:09:40,160
up by some orthographical stumbling-block; but on the whole he got on very well indeed;

105
00:09:40,160 --> 00:09:45,038
and when he had signed his name, and had removed a finishing blot from the paper to the crown of

106
00:09:45,038 --> 00:09:51,078
his head with his two forefingers, he got up and hovered about the table, trying the effect

107
00:09:51,078 --> 00:09:58,116
of his performance from various points of view, as it lay there, with unbounded satisfaction.

108
00:09:58,116 --> 00:10:01,038
Not to make Joe uneasy by talking too much,

109
00:10:01,038 --> 00:10:05,398
even if I had been able to talk much, I deferred asking him about Miss Havisham

110
00:10:05,398 --> 00:10:11,436
until next day. He shook his head when I then asked him if she had recovered.

111
00:10:11,438 --> 00:10:12,438
“Is she dead, Joe?”

112
00:10:13,519 --> 00:10:18,796
“Why you see, old chap,” said Joe, in a tone of remonstrance, and by way of getting at it

113
00:10:18,796 --> 00:10:26,116
by degrees, “I wouldn’t go so far as to say that, for that’s a deal to say; but she ain’t—”

114
00:10:26,116 --> 00:10:27,917
“Living, Joe?”

115
00:10:27,917 --> 00:10:32,160
“That’s nigher where it is,” said Joe; “she ain’t living.”

116
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:34,718
“Did she linger long, Joe?”

117
00:10:34,718 --> 00:10:39,116
“Arter you was took ill, pretty much about what you might call (if you was put to it)

118
00:10:39,116 --> 00:10:45,319
a week,” said Joe; still determined, on my account, to come at everything by degrees.

119
00:10:45,319 --> 00:10:49,358
“Dear Joe, have you heard what becomes of her property?”

120
00:10:49,358 --> 00:10:54,557
“Well, old chap,” said Joe, “it do appear that she had settled the most of it, which

121
00:10:54,559 --> 00:11:01,319
I meantersay tied it up, on Miss Estella. But she had wrote out a little coddleshell in her own hand

122
00:11:01,319 --> 00:11:08,436
a day or two afore the accident, leaving a cool four thousand to Mr. Matthew Pocket. And why,

123
00:11:08,438 --> 00:11:14,358
do you suppose, above all things, Pip, she left that cool four thousand unto him? ‘Because of

124
00:11:14,358 --> 00:11:21,038
Pip’s account of him, the said Matthew.’ I am told by Biddy, that air the writing,” said Joe,

125
00:11:21,038 --> 00:11:26,476
repeating the legal turn as if it did him infinite good, “‘account of him the said

126
00:11:26,476 --> 00:11:34,038
Matthew.’ And a cool four thousand, Pip!” I never discovered from whom Joe derived

127
00:11:34,038 --> 00:11:38,398
the conventional temperature of the four thousand pounds; but it appeared to make

128
00:11:38,398 --> 00:11:43,917
the sum of money more to him, and he had a manifest relish in insisting on its being cool.

129
00:11:43,917 --> 00:11:47,559
This account gave me great joy, as it perfected the only good thing I

130
00:11:47,559 --> 00:11:53,839
had done. I asked Joe whether he had heard if any of the other relations had any legacies?

131
00:11:53,839 --> 00:11:57,839
“Miss Sarah,” said Joe, “she have twenty-five pound perannium fur

132
00:11:57,839 --> 00:12:02,519
to buy pills, on account of being bilious. Miss Georgiana,

133
00:12:02,519 --> 00:12:09,839
she have twenty pound down. Mrs.—what’s the name of them wild beasts with humps, old chap?”

134
00:12:09,839 --> 00:12:14,637
“Camels?” said I, wondering why he could possibly want to know.

135
00:12:14,637 --> 00:12:21,476
Joe nodded. “Mrs. Camels,” by which I presently understood he meant Camilla,

136
00:12:21,476 --> 00:12:27,879
“she have five pound fur to buy rushlights to put her in spirits when she wake up in the night.”

137
00:12:27,879 --> 00:12:33,319
The accuracy of these recitals was sufficiently obvious to me, to give me great confidence in

138
00:12:33,319 --> 00:12:40,917
Joe’s information. “And now,” said Joe, “you ain’t that strong yet, old chap, that you can take in

139
00:12:40,917 --> 00:12:48,199
more nor one additional shovelful to-day. Old Orlick he’s been a bustin’ open a dwelling-ouse.”

140
00:12:48,199 --> 00:12:50,678
“Whose?” said I.

141
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:56,559
“Not, I grant you, but what his manners is given to blusterous,” said Joe, apologetically; “still,

142
00:12:56,559 --> 00:13:02,398
a Englishman’s ouse is his Castle, and castles must not be busted ’cept when done in war time.

143
00:13:02,398 --> 00:13:08,796
And wotsume’er the failings on his part, he were a corn and seedsman in his hart.”

144
00:13:08,796 --> 00:13:12,237
“Is it Pumblechook’s house that has been broken into, then?”

145
00:13:12,917 --> 00:13:19,038
“That’s it, Pip,” said Joe; “and they took his till, and they took his cash-box,

146
00:13:19,038 --> 00:13:24,718
and they drinked his wine, and they partook of his wittles, and they slapped his face,

147
00:13:24,718 --> 00:13:30,398
and they pulled his nose, and they tied him up to his bedpust, and they giv’ him a dozen,

148
00:13:30,398 --> 00:13:33,319
and they stuffed his mouth full of flowering annuals to prewent his

149
00:13:33,319 --> 00:13:38,879
crying out. But he knowed Orlick, and Orlick’s in the county jail.”

150
00:13:38,879 --> 00:13:42,237
By these approaches we arrived at unrestricted conversation.

151
00:13:43,199 --> 00:13:47,917
I was slow to gain strength, but I did slowly and surely become less weak,

152
00:13:47,917 --> 00:13:51,957
and Joe stayed with me, and I fancied I was little Pip again.

153
00:13:51,957 --> 00:13:56,718
For the tenderness of Joe was so beautifully proportioned to my need, that I was like a

154
00:13:56,718 --> 00:14:04,160
child in his hands. He would sit and talk to me in the old confidence, and with the old simplicity,

155
00:14:04,160 --> 00:14:09,839
and in the old unassertive protecting way, so that I would half believe that all my life since the

156
00:14:09,839 --> 00:14:15,758
days of the old kitchen was one of the mental troubles of the fever that was gone. He did

157
00:14:15,758 --> 00:14:21,519
everything for me except the household work, for which he had engaged a very decent woman, after

158
00:14:21,519 --> 00:14:27,597
paying off the laundress on his first arrival. “Which I do assure you, Pip,” he would often say,

159
00:14:27,597 --> 00:14:34,476
in explanation of that liberty; “I found her a tapping the spare bed, like a cask of beer,

160
00:14:34,476 --> 00:14:39,476
and drawing off the feathers in a bucket, for sale. Which she would have tapped yourn next,

161
00:14:39,476 --> 00:14:45,038
and draw’d it off with you a laying on it, and was then a carrying away the coals gradiwally

162
00:14:45,038 --> 00:14:51,000
in the soup-tureen and wegetable-dishes, and the wine and spirits in your Wellington boots.”

163
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,917
We looked forward to the day when I should go out for a ride,

164
00:14:53,917 --> 00:14:58,796
as we had once looked forward to the day of my apprenticeship. And when the day came,

165
00:14:58,796 --> 00:15:04,718
and an open carriage was got into the Lane, Joe wrapped me up, took me in his arms,

166
00:15:04,718 --> 00:15:09,319
carried me down to it, and put me in, as if I were still the small helpless

167
00:15:09,319 --> 00:15:14,635
creature to whom he had so abundantly given of the wealth of his great nature.

168
00:15:14,637 --> 00:15:19,559
And Joe got in beside me, and we drove away together into the country, where the rich

169
00:15:19,559 --> 00:15:26,438
summer growth was already on the trees and on the grass, and sweet summer scents filled all

170
00:15:26,438 --> 00:15:34,277
the air. The day happened to be Sunday, and when I looked on the loveliness around me,

171
00:15:34,277 --> 00:15:39,879
and thought how it had grown and changed, and how the little wild-flowers had been forming, and the

172
00:15:39,879 --> 00:15:47,358
voices of the birds had been strengthening, by day and by night, under the sun and under the stars,

173
00:15:47,358 --> 00:15:53,519
while poor I lay burning and tossing on my bed, the mere remembrance of having burned and tossed

174
00:15:53,519 --> 00:15:59,559
there came like a check upon my peace. But when I heard the Sunday bells, and looked around a

175
00:15:59,559 --> 00:16:06,839
little more upon the outspread beauty, I felt that I was not nearly thankful enough,—that I

176
00:16:06,839 --> 00:16:13,237
was too weak yet to be even that,—and I laid my head on Joe’s shoulder, as I had laid it long

177
00:16:13,237 --> 00:16:20,758
ago when he had taken me to the Fair or where not, and it was too much for my young senses.

178
00:16:20,758 --> 00:16:25,277
More composure came to me after a while, and we talked as we used to talk,

179
00:16:25,277 --> 00:16:31,436
lying on the grass at the old Battery. There was no change whatever in Joe. Exactly what he had

180
00:16:31,438 --> 00:16:40,277
been in my eyes then, he was in my eyes still; just as simply faithful, and as simply right.

181
00:16:40,277 --> 00:16:45,597
When we got back again, and he lifted me out, and carried me—so easily!—across the court and up the

182
00:16:45,597 --> 00:16:51,637
stairs, I thought of that eventful Christmas Day when he had carried me over the marshes.

183
00:16:51,637 --> 00:16:56,917
We had not yet made any allusion to my change of fortune, nor did I know how much of my late

184
00:16:56,917 --> 00:17:04,116
history he was acquainted with. I was so doubtful of myself now, and put so much trust in him,

185
00:17:04,116 --> 00:17:09,557
that I could not satisfy myself whether I ought to refer to it when he did not.

186
00:17:09,559 --> 00:17:12,358
“Have you heard, Joe,” I asked him that evening,

187
00:17:12,358 --> 00:17:19,199
upon further consideration, as he smoked his pipe at the window, “who my patron was?”

188
00:17:19,199 --> 00:17:24,237
“I heerd,” returned Joe, “as it were not Miss Havisham, old chap.”

189
00:17:24,237 --> 00:17:26,635
“Did you hear who it was, Joe?”

190
00:17:26,637 --> 00:17:29,758
“Well! I heerd as it were a person what sent the person

191
00:17:29,758 --> 00:17:32,718
what giv’ you the bank-notes at the Jolly Bargemen, Pip.”

192
00:17:32,718 --> 00:17:34,199
“So it was.”

193
00:17:34,199 --> 00:17:38,116
“Astonishing!” said Joe, in the placidest way.

194
00:17:38,116 --> 00:17:41,917
“Did you hear that he was dead, Joe?” I presently asked, with increasing diffidence.

195
00:17:43,038 --> 00:17:46,160
“Which? Him as sent the bank-notes, Pip?”

196
00:17:46,160 --> 00:17:47,680
“Yes.”

197
00:17:47,680 --> 00:17:54,796
“I think,” said Joe, after meditating a long time, and looking rather evasively at the window-seat,

198
00:17:54,796 --> 00:18:01,597
“as I did hear tell that how he were something or another in a general way in that direction.”

199
00:18:01,597 --> 00:18:05,038
“Did you hear anything of his circumstances, Joe?”

200
00:18:05,038 --> 00:18:07,277
“Not partickler, Pip.”

201
00:18:07,277 --> 00:18:13,678
“If you would like to hear, Joe—” I was beginning, when Joe got up and came to my sofa.

202
00:18:13,680 --> 00:18:19,879
“Lookee here, old chap,” said Joe, bending over me. “Ever the best of friends; ain’t us, Pip?”

203
00:18:19,879 --> 00:18:22,237
I was ashamed to answer him.

204
00:18:22,237 --> 00:18:26,519
“Wery good, then,” said Joe, as if I had answered; “that’s all right;

205
00:18:26,519 --> 00:18:32,519
that’s agreed upon. Then why go into subjects, old chap, which as betwixt two sech must be for

206
00:18:32,519 --> 00:18:38,476
ever onnecessary? There’s subjects enough as betwixt two sech, without onnecessary ones.

207
00:18:38,476 --> 00:18:45,438
Lord! To think of your poor sister and her Rampages! And don’t you remember Tickler?”

208
00:18:45,438 --> 00:18:47,277
“I do indeed, Joe.”

209
00:18:47,277 --> 00:18:52,756
“Lookee here, old chap,” said Joe. “I done what I could to keep you and Tickler in sunders,

210
00:18:52,758 --> 00:18:57,559
but my power were not always fully equal to my inclinations. For when your poor sister had a

211
00:18:57,559 --> 00:19:03,758
mind to drop into you, it were not so much,” said Joe, in his favourite argumentative way,

212
00:19:03,758 --> 00:19:08,637
“that she dropped into me too, if I put myself in opposition to her, but that she dropped

213
00:19:08,637 --> 00:19:15,038
into you always heavier for it. I noticed that. It ain’t a grab at a man’s whisker,

214
00:19:15,038 --> 00:19:20,680
not yet a shake or two of a man (to which your sister was quite welcome), that ’ud put a man

215
00:19:20,680 --> 00:19:26,199
off from getting a little child out of punishment. But when that little child is dropped into heavier

216
00:19:26,199 --> 00:19:31,557
for that grab of whisker or shaking, then that man naterally up and says to himself,

217
00:19:31,559 --> 00:19:36,839
‘Where is the good as you are a-doing? I grant you I see the ’arm,’ says the man,

218
00:19:36,839 --> 00:19:41,839
‘but I don’t see the good. I call upon you, sir, therefore, to pint out the good.’”

219
00:19:42,398 --> 00:19:46,597
“The man says?” I observed, as Joe waited for me to speak.

220
00:19:46,597 --> 00:19:51,000
“The man says,” Joe assented. “Is he right, that man?”

221
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:54,358
“Dear Joe, he is always right.”

222
00:19:54,358 --> 00:19:59,957
“Well, old chap,” said Joe, “then abide by your words. If he’s always right (which in general

223
00:19:59,957 --> 00:20:06,597
he’s more likely wrong), he’s right when he says this: Supposing ever you kep any little matter to

224
00:20:06,597 --> 00:20:12,000
yourself, when you was a little child, you kep it mostly because you know’d as J. Gargery’s

225
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:18,358
power to part you and Tickler in sunders were not fully equal to his inclinations. Theerfore,

226
00:20:18,358 --> 00:20:23,519
think no more of it as betwixt two sech, and do not let us pass remarks upon onnecessary

227
00:20:23,519 --> 00:20:29,879
subjects. Biddy giv’ herself a deal o’ trouble with me afore I left (for I am almost awful dull),

228
00:20:29,879 --> 00:20:33,116
as I should view it in this light, and, viewing it in this light,

229
00:20:33,116 --> 00:20:39,879
as I should so put it. Both of which,” said Joe, quite charmed with his logical arrangement,

230
00:20:39,879 --> 00:20:46,038
“being done, now this to you a true friend, say. Namely. You mustn’t go a overdoing on it,

231
00:20:46,038 --> 00:20:51,839
but you must have your supper and your wine and water, and you must be put betwixt the sheets.”

232
00:20:51,839 --> 00:20:56,237
The delicacy with which Joe dismissed this theme, and the sweet tact and kindness

233
00:20:56,237 --> 00:21:02,237
with which Biddy—who with her woman’s wit had found me out so soon—had prepared him for it,

234
00:21:02,237 --> 00:21:09,078
made a deep impression on my mind. But whether Joe knew how poor I was, and how my great expectations

235
00:21:09,078 --> 00:21:15,957
had all dissolved, like our own marsh mists before the sun, I could not understand.

236
00:21:15,957 --> 00:21:21,199
Another thing in Joe that I could not understand when it first began to develop itself, but which

237
00:21:21,199 --> 00:21:29,358
I soon arrived at a sorrowful comprehension of, was this: As I became stronger and better,

238
00:21:29,358 --> 00:21:34,436
Joe became a little less easy with me. In my weakness and entire dependence on him,

239
00:21:34,438 --> 00:21:38,718
the dear fellow had fallen into the old tone, and called me by the old names,

240
00:21:38,718 --> 00:21:45,917
the dear “old Pip, old chap,” that now were music in my ears. I too had fallen into the old ways,

241
00:21:45,917 --> 00:21:52,038
only happy and thankful that he let me. But, imperceptibly, though I held by them fast,

242
00:21:52,038 --> 00:21:58,358
Joe’s hold upon them began to slacken; and whereas I wondered at this, at first,

243
00:21:58,358 --> 00:22:05,199
I soon began to understand that the cause of it was in me, and that the fault of it was all mine.

244
00:22:05,199 --> 00:22:10,557
Ah! Had I given Joe no reason to doubt my constancy, and to think that in prosperity

245
00:22:10,559 --> 00:22:15,358
I should grow cold to him and cast him off? Had I given Joe’s innocent heart

246
00:22:15,358 --> 00:22:21,436
no cause to feel instinctively that as I got stronger, his hold upon me would be weaker,

247
00:22:21,438 --> 00:22:27,796
and that he had better loosen it in time and let me go, before I plucked myself away?

248
00:22:27,796 --> 00:22:32,078
It was on the third or fourth occasion of my going out walking in the Temple Gardens

249
00:22:32,078 --> 00:22:38,597
leaning on Joe’s arm, that I saw this change in him very plainly. We had been sitting in

250
00:22:38,597 --> 00:22:44,559
the bright warm sunlight, looking at the river, and I chanced to say as we got up,—

251
00:22:44,559 --> 00:22:51,078
“See, Joe! I can walk quite strongly. Now, you shall see me walk back by myself.”

252
00:22:51,078 --> 00:22:57,398
“Which do not overdo it, Pip,” said Joe; “but I shall be happy fur to see you able, sir.”

253
00:22:57,398 --> 00:23:03,319
The last word grated on me; but how could I remonstrate! I walked no further than

254
00:23:03,319 --> 00:23:07,078
the gate of the gardens, and then pretended to be weaker than I was,

255
00:23:07,078 --> 00:23:12,000
and asked Joe for his arm. Joe gave it me, but was thoughtful.

256
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:17,519
I, for my part, was thoughtful too; for, how best to check this growing change in Joe was a

257
00:23:17,519 --> 00:23:23,796
great perplexity to my remorseful thoughts. That I was ashamed to tell him exactly how I was placed,

258
00:23:23,796 --> 00:23:29,160
and what I had come down to, I do not seek to conceal; but I hope my reluctance was

259
00:23:29,160 --> 00:23:34,796
not quite an unworthy one. He would want to help me out of his little savings, I knew,

260
00:23:34,796 --> 00:23:39,438
and I knew that he ought not to help me, and that I must not suffer him to do it.

261
00:23:39,438 --> 00:23:45,237
It was a thoughtful evening with both of us. But, before we went to bed, I had resolved that I would

262
00:23:45,237 --> 00:23:52,358
wait over to-morrow,—to-morrow being Sunday,—and would begin my new course with the new week. On

263
00:23:52,358 --> 00:23:57,436
Monday morning I would speak to Joe about this change, I would lay aside this last vestige

264
00:23:57,438 --> 00:24:03,796
of reserve, I would tell him what I had in my thoughts (that Secondly, not yet arrived at), and

265
00:24:03,796 --> 00:24:10,398
why I had not decided to go out to Herbert, and then the change would be conquered for ever. As

266
00:24:10,398 --> 00:24:18,116
I cleared, Joe cleared, and it seemed as though he had sympathetically arrived at a resolution too.

267
00:24:18,116 --> 00:24:25,000
We had a quiet day on the Sunday, and we rode out into the country, and then walked in the fields.

268
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:28,476
“I feel thankful that I have been ill, Joe,” I said.

269
00:24:28,476 --> 00:24:32,680
“Dear old Pip, old chap, you’re a’most come round, sir.”

270
00:24:32,680 --> 00:24:35,637
“It has been a memorable time for me, Joe.”

271
00:24:35,637 --> 00:24:38,758
“Likeways for myself, sir,” Joe returned.

272
00:24:38,758 --> 00:24:41,438
“We have had a time together, Joe, that I can never forget.

273
00:24:42,476 --> 00:24:49,680
There were days once, I know, that I did for a while forget; but I never shall forget these.”

274
00:24:49,680 --> 00:24:53,718
“Pip,” said Joe, appearing a little hurried and troubled,

275
00:24:53,718 --> 00:25:00,319
“there has been larks. And, dear sir, what have been betwixt us—have been.”

276
00:25:00,319 --> 00:25:05,237
At night, when I had gone to bed, Joe came into my room, as he had done all

277
00:25:05,237 --> 00:25:11,199
through my recovery. He asked me if I felt sure that I was as well as in the morning?

278
00:25:11,199 --> 00:25:13,557
“Yes, dear Joe, quite.”

279
00:25:13,559 --> 00:25:16,680
“And are always a getting stronger, old chap?”

280
00:25:16,680 --> 00:25:18,917
“Yes, dear Joe, steadily.”

281
00:25:18,917 --> 00:25:23,199
Joe patted the coverlet on my shoulder with his great good hand, and said,

282
00:25:23,199 --> 00:25:28,398
in what I thought a husky voice, “Good night!”

283
00:25:28,398 --> 00:25:33,557
When I got up in the morning, refreshed and stronger yet, I was full of my resolution to

284
00:25:33,559 --> 00:25:39,559
tell Joe all, without delay. I would tell him before breakfast. I would dress at once and

285
00:25:39,559 --> 00:25:45,637
go to his room and surprise him; for, it was the first day I had been up early. I went to his room,

286
00:25:45,637 --> 00:25:51,879
and he was not there. Not only was he not there, but his box was gone.

287
00:25:51,879 --> 00:25:57,796
I hurried then to the breakfast-table, and on it found a letter. These were its brief contents:—

288
00:25:57,796 --> 00:26:01,078
“Not wishful to intrude I have departured fur

289
00:26:01,078 --> 00:26:04,796
you are well again dear Pip and will do better without.

290
00:26:04,796 --> 00:26:05,357
Jo.

291
00:26:05,357 --> 00:26:07,618
“P.S. Ever the best of friends.”

292
00:26:07,618 --> 00:26:08,358
-

293
00:26:08,358 --> 00:26:11,957
Enclosed in the letter was a receipt for the debt and costs on which I had been

294
00:26:11,957 --> 00:26:18,199
arrested. Down to that moment, I had vainly supposed that my creditor had withdrawn,

295
00:26:18,199 --> 00:26:22,718
or suspended proceedings until I should be quite recovered. I had never dreamed

296
00:26:22,718 --> 00:26:30,038
of Joe’s having paid the money; but Joe had paid it, and the receipt was in his name.

297
00:26:30,038 --> 00:26:35,917
What remained for me now, but to follow him to the dear old forge, and there to have out my

298
00:26:35,917 --> 00:26:42,277
disclosure to him, and my penitent remonstrance with him, and there to relieve my mind and heart

299
00:26:42,277 --> 00:26:48,038
of that reserved Secondly, which had begun as a vague something lingering in my thoughts,

300
00:26:48,038 --> 00:26:54,597
and had formed into a settled purpose? The purpose was, that I would go to Biddy,

301
00:26:54,597 --> 00:27:00,718
that I would show her how humbled and repentant I came back, that I would tell her how I had lost

302
00:27:00,718 --> 00:27:09,160
all I once hoped for, that I would remind her of our old confidences in my first unhappy time. Then

303
00:27:09,160 --> 00:27:17,038
I would say to her, “Biddy, I think you once liked me very well, when my errant heart, even while it

304
00:27:17,038 --> 00:27:23,680
strayed away from you, was quieter and better with you than it ever has been since. If you can like

305
00:27:23,680 --> 00:27:29,957
me only half as well once more, if you can take me with all my faults and disappointments on my head,

306
00:27:29,957 --> 00:27:36,839
if you can receive me like a forgiven child (and indeed I am as sorry, Biddy, and have as much need

307
00:27:36,839 --> 00:27:44,796
of a hushing voice and a soothing hand), I hope I am a little worthier of you that I was,—not much,

308
00:27:44,796 --> 00:27:49,319
but a little. And, Biddy, it shall rest with you to say whether I shall work at the forge

309
00:27:49,319 --> 00:27:54,436
with Joe, or whether I shall try for any different occupation down in this country,

310
00:27:54,438 --> 00:27:59,879
or whether we shall go away to a distant place where an opportunity awaits me which I set aside,

311
00:27:59,879 --> 00:28:07,839
when it was offered, until I knew your answer. And now, dear Biddy, if you can tell me that

312
00:28:07,839 --> 00:28:13,559
you will go through the world with me, you will surely make it a better world for me,

313
00:28:13,559 --> 00:28:20,758
and me a better man for it, and I will try hard to make it a better world for you.”

314
00:28:20,758 --> 00:28:26,199
Such was my purpose. After three days more of recovery,

315
00:28:26,199 --> 00:28:31,237
I went down to the old place to put it in execution. And how I sped in it is all I

316
00:28:31,237 --> 00:28:34,000
have left to tell. Chapter 58.
